dict(sec=15, img=None,
stem="Three patients are admitted after transmural myocardial infarctions. Patient 1 deteriorates 6 hours after onset, patient 2 on day 4, and patient 3 at week 7." + hdr('Patient 1 (6 hours)','Patient 2 (day 4)','Patient 3 (week 7)') + "{FIG}Which of the following sets of complications best matches each time point?",
choices=["Dressler syndrome, ventricular fibrillation, free-wall rupture","Free-wall rupture, true aneurysm, early pericarditis","Papillary muscle rupture, Dressler syndrome, ventricular fibrillation","True aneurysm, early pericarditis, ventricular septal rupture","Ventricular fibrillation, free-wall rupture, true aneurysm"], key=4,
exp="Timing usually gives the answer:<br>• <b>0–24 hours</b> (coagulative necrosis beginning): <b>ventricular arrhythmia</b> and sudden death, bradycardia and atrioventricular block, acute heart failure, cardiogenic shock.<br>• <b>1–3 days</b> (neutrophils): early fibrinous pericarditis.<br>• <b>3–14 days</b> (macrophages, softest wall): <b>ruptures</b> — free wall (tamponade), papillary muscle (acute mitral regurgitation), septum (left-to-right shunt), pseudoaneurysm.<br>• <b>2 weeks to months</b> (granulation tissue, then scar): <b>true aneurysm</b>, mural thrombus, Dressler syndrome, chronic heart failure.<br>Arrhythmias, heart failure and re-infarction can happen at any time.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Match complications to time: arrhythmia in the first day, rupture at 3–14 days, aneurysm and Dressler syndrome after 2 weeks.",
wrong={0:"Dressler syndrome is a weeks-later complication, and free-wall rupture does not occur at week 7.",
1:"Rupture needs days of softening, and early pericarditis belongs to days 1–3, not week 7.",
2:"Ventricular fibrillation at week 7 is possible but not characteristic, and Dressler syndrome does not occur on day 4.",
3:"An aneurysm does not form within 6 hours."},
eli5="First day: the heart's electrics go haywire. First week: the soft wall can tear. After a few weeks: the scar can bulge out."),
